std.strconv — Numeric and boolean string conversion
=====================================================

The `std.strconv` module parses and formats primitive values
as decimal strings. It is the second-lowest text-handling
module in the standard library: it depends only on `std.mem`
for byte manipulation, and is itself the foundation for
`std.builder` (which uses it for numeric interpolation) and
for user code that needs to read or write simple numeric text.

API shape:

- **Parsers** take a byte slice and return `(value, ok)`. The
  boolean `ok` is `true` iff the entire input was consumed as
  a well-formed value. This mirrors Go's `strconv.ParseInt` in
  outcome, but uses a plain boolean rather than an error type,
  matching the convention established by `std.utf8.decode_rune`.
  When `ok` is `false`, the returned value is zero (or `false`
  for `parse_bool`).

- **Formatters** return a freshly-allocated `string` containing
  the decimal representation. A second, lower-level form
  (`format_*_into`) writes into a caller-supplied buffer and
  returns the number of bytes written, which is what
  `std.builder` will call internally to avoid an allocation.

When Sysl gains a `Result[T, E]` type and `std` adopts it
uniformly, the `(value, ok)` tuple will be replaced by
`Result[T, ParseError]`. The API shape is forward-compatible:
callers pattern-match `ok` today and will pattern-match
`Ok(v) | Err(e)` later.

Dependencies: `std.mem` (not directly used in this file, but
conceptually part of the layering).


    module std.strconv


Parsers
-------

### parse_bool

`parse_bool(s)` recognizes exactly `"true"`, `"false"`, `"1"`,
and `"0"`. No leading or trailing whitespace, no alternate
casings (`"True"`, `"TRUE"`, `"yes"`, `"no"` all reject). The
strict set keeps this module unopinionated — callers that
want lenient behavior can normalize their input first.

    parse_bool(s: []byte) -> (bool, bool)
        val n = len(s)

        if n == 1
            if s[0] == byte('1') then return true, true
            if s[0] == byte('0') then return false, true
            return false, false

        if n == 4 && s[0] == byte('t') && s[1] == byte('r') && s[2] == byte('u') && s[3] == byte('e') then return true, true
        if n == 5 && s[0] == byte('f') && s[1] == byte('a') && s[2] == byte('l') && s[3] == byte('s') && s[4] == byte('e') then return false, true

        false, false


### parse_i64

`parse_i64(s)` parses a decimal integer with an optional
leading `+` or `-`. At least one digit is required. The entire
input must be consumed; trailing non-digits cause failure.

The full 64-bit signed range `[-2^63, 2^63-1]` is supported,
including `MIN_I64` (which cannot be represented as a positive
magnitude). This is achieved by accumulating the value as a
negative number: `|MIN_I64|` is one larger than `MAX_I64`, so
negative-accumulation covers the whole range with one code path.

    parse_i64(s: []byte) -> (i64, bool)
        val n = len(s)

        if n == 0 then return 0i64, false

        var i = 0
        var neg = false

        if s[0] == byte('+')
            i = 1
        else if s[0] == byte('-')
            neg = true
            i = 1

        if i >= n then return 0i64, false

        // Accumulate as a negative i64 to cover MIN_I64.
        // LIMIT = MIN_I64 / 10, truncated toward zero.
        var acc: i64 = 0i64
        val LIMIT: i64 = -922337203685477580i64

        while i < n
            val b = s[i]

            if b < byte('0') || b > byte('9') then return 0i64, false

            val d: i64 = i64(b - byte('0'))

            if acc < LIMIT then return 0i64, false
            if acc == LIMIT && d > 8i64 then return 0i64, false

            acc = acc * 10i64 - d
            i++

        if neg then return acc, true

        // Positive: negate. If acc == MIN_I64, -acc would overflow.
        if acc < -9223372036854775807i64 then return 0i64, false

        -acc, true


### parse_int

`parse_int(s)` is `parse_i64` narrowed to the 32-bit signed
range. Values outside `[MIN_I32, MAX_I32]` fail with
`(0, false)` even if they are valid i64 values.

    parse_int(s: []byte) -> (int, bool)
        val v, ok = parse_i64(s)

        if !ok then return 0, false
        if v > 2147483647i64 then return 0, false
        if v < -2147483648i64 then return 0, false

        int(v), true


Formatters
----------

### format_bool

`format_bool(b)` returns `"true"` or `"false"` as a string.

    format_bool(b: bool) -> string
        if b then "true" else "false"


### format_i64_into

`format_i64_into(dst, n)` writes the decimal representation of
`n` into `dst` starting at index 0, and returns the number of
bytes written. Returns `0` if `dst` is too small (maximum of
20 bytes is required, for `-9223372036854775808`).

Digits are generated by repeated division. To handle `MIN_I64`,
the value is accumulated negatively throughout, so `-n` is
never computed on a positive overflow path.

    format_i64_into(dst: []byte, n: i64) -> int
        val cap = len(dst)

        if cap < 1 then return 0

        // Accumulate as a negative number to cover MIN_I64.
        var x: i64 = n

        if x > 0i64 then x = -x

        // Write digits to a temporary buffer in reverse.
        // Max 19 digits + optional sign = 20 bytes.
        val tmp = new [20]byte
        var i = 20

        if x == 0i64
            i--
            tmp[i] = byte('0')
        else
            while x < 0i64
                i--

                val d: i64 = -(x - (x / 10i64) * 10i64)

                tmp[i] = byte(int(byte('0')) + int(d))
                x = x / 10i64

        if n < 0i64
            i--
            tmp[i] = byte('-')

        val written: int = 20 - i

        if written > cap then return 0

        for j = 0; j < written; j++ do dst[j] = tmp[i + j]

        written


### format_i64

`format_i64(n)` returns the decimal representation of `n` as
a freshly-allocated `string`.

    format_i64(n: i64) -> string
        val buf = new [20]byte
        val written = format_i64_into(buf, n)

        string(buf[:written])


### format_int

`format_int(n)` is `format_i64` on an `int` value.

    format_int(n: int) -> string = format_i64(i64(n))


Design notes
------------

**Why `(value, ok)` and not `Result[T, E]`.** Sum types exist
in Sysl, and a `Result` type is eventually the right shape for
parse errors carrying location and reason. But introducing a
`std.result.Result[T, E]` type is a stdlib-wide commitment —
every parser, every I/O op, every operation that can fail
wants to use it consistently. Rather than choose it piecemeal,
`std.strconv` uses the simpler tuple form now and migrates
when the `Result` type is formally introduced.

**Why strict parsing.** `parse_i64(" 42 ")` fails, not succeeds.
This module is a primitive; lenient behavior (whitespace
trimming, alternate radices, thousands separators) belongs in
higher-level modules that explicitly opt in. A strict primitive
can always be wrapped; a lenient one cannot be unwrapped.

**Why parse_bool accepts "1"/"0".** Many data sources (CLI
flags, CSV, config files) emit `1`/`0` for booleans. Accepting
both pays for itself immediately. Rejecting `"True"`/`"TRUE"`
is deliberate: locale-dependent or case-insensitive matching
belongs outside the primitive.

**No radix support yet.** Hex, octal, and binary parsing will
appear as `parse_i64_radix(s, base)` when needed. Not planning
for Go's `ParseInt(s, base, bitSize)` surface — base is
usually known at compile time, and callers can wrap as needed.

**No floating-point yet.** `parse_f64` / `format_f64` require
IEEE 754 rounding, shortest-representation algorithms
(Grisu3/Ryu), and scientific notation — enough complexity to
justify their own module (`std.strconv.float` or similar).
Deferred until there's concrete demand from user code.

**Allocation in format_i64.** The public formatters allocate.
Callers in hot paths should use `format_i64_into` with a
caller-owned buffer; `std.builder` will do exactly this. The
allocation is small (up to 20 bytes, ref-counted), so it is
fine for most uses.
